    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_SweepRange">Glides from 20 Hz to 20 kHz, then repeats.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_NoisePink">Pink</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_NoiseWhite">White</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_PhaseIn">In phase</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_PhaseOut">Out of phase</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_PhaseAlt">Switch</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_PhaseClick">Click</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_ChannelsNote">Each channel in turn: one beep for the first, two for the second, and so on. Click a tile to leave a channel out.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_TestChannels">Channels</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_TestPhase">Phase</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_TestTone">Tone</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_TestSweep">Sweep</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_TestNoise">Noise</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_ChannelsHeader">Channels (click to include or leave out)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_TileTip">Click to include or leave out this channel</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_AllTip">Switch every channel back on</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_All">All channels</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Play">Play</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Stop">Stop</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Stopped">Stopped</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_OpenFailed">Couldn't open the device: {0}</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_StateIn">In phase: the sound should sit in the middle</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_StateOut">Out of phase: the sound should feel wide and hollow</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_StateIdle">Press Play</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_ClickNormal">Click 1: both sides push the same way</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_ClickInverted">Click 2: the right side is flipped</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Channels">You should hear every channel on its own, in order, with the right number of beeps and at about the same loudness. A channel that is silent, quiet, crackly or on the wrong side points to a bad connector, a damaged cable or a wrong speaker setup. On headphones, one beep must be in the left ear and two in the right.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Phase_InPhase">Both sides play exactly the same. The sound should appear in the middle of your head (or between the speakers). If it seems to come from one side, or wide and vague, one side may be wired in reverse or the balance is off.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Phase_Out">The right side is flipped upside down. This is meant to sound wide, hollow and thin in the bass, and hard to place. Compare it with In phase: if In phase sounds like this, one of your drivers is wired backwards.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Phase_Alt">Switches between In phase and Out of phase every three seconds. In phase should sound solid and centered; Out of phase should sound wider and thinner. If they sound the same, one side may be silent or the output is mono.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Phase_Click">One click a second. The first click pushes both drivers the same way; the second flips the right one. On a speaker or a driver you can see or feel, clicks 1 and 2 on both sides should move the same way on click 1. A reverse-wired driver moves the other way. Best checked with a mic, a scope, or by eye on a woofer.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Tone">A steady tone. Use it to check one frequency: a rattle, buzz or hum, a channel that is much quieter at one pitch, or hearing limits (older ears and small in-ears fade out above 15 to 18 kHz). Square, triangle and saw add harmonics and sound brighter.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Sweep">A slow glide from deep bass to the highest treble. Listen for dips and bumps in loudness, rattles, buzzes, a sudden cut, or one side disappearing at some pitch. Leave only one channel on to find which side is the problem.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Guide_Noise">Steady hiss, different on every channel. Pink noise sounds even across the range and is best for comparing left and right loudness. White noise is brighter. Both sides should sound equally loud and equally wide. Turn channels off to compare one at a time.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Spk_L">Left</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Spk_R">Right</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_AudioOut_Spk_M">Mono</system:String>
