    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_ShowHints">Show key hints on screen</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Tips">Wait a few minutes after switching the monitor on. Clean the screen first, so dust isn't taken for a bad pixel. In the test: arrow keys change the picture, a click marks a suspect pixel, Esc ends.</system:String>

    <!--  Monitor HDR & Color Depth Checker (monitor-hdr)  -->
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Header">HDR &amp; color depth</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_ScreenHeader">SCREEN</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_ScreenTip">Choose the screen to read and to run the test pictures on</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Refresh">Read the screens again (after turning HDR on or off, or changing the cable)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Copy">Copy</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Save">Save</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Copied">Readings copied</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Saved">Report saved</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_ClipboardBusy">The clipboard is busy, try again</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Intro">Click a picture to open it full screen on the chosen screen. Below are the readings Windows gives for that screen, and what they mean.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_ReadingsHeader">Readings</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_VerdictHeader">What this means</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_NoData">Windows reported no screens.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Limits">The numbers come from Windows and the screen's own data (EDID), not from a measuring device, so brightness and color range are what the screen claims. The test pictures are 8-bit: they show banding and clipping, but the bit depth shown above is what the cable and port really carry.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Keys">← → change picture  ·  Space slideshow  ·  ↑ ↓ white window size  ·  H hints  ·  Esc exit</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_ReportTitle">HDR &amp; color depth report</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Unknown">Not reported</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_On">On</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Off">Off</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_NotSupported">Not supported</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Resolution">Resolution and refresh</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Monitor">Monitor</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_BitDepth">Bits per color (now)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PanelMax">Screen's maximum</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_ColorFormat">Color format</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Hdr">HDR</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PeakNits">Peak brightness</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_FullFrameNits">Full-screen brightness</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_MinNits">Black level</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_SdrWhite">SDR content brightness</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Size">size</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_GamutValue">{0:0}% covered (screen is {1:0}% of its size)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_no_hdr">This screen does not report HDR support. Everything here still works in SDR.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_hdr_off">HDR is supported but turned off in Windows. Turn on "Use HDR" in Settings &gt; System &gt; Display.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_hdr_dim">HDR is on, but the screen only reaches about {0:0} nits, so highlights will look modest (real HDR starts around 400 nits).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_hdr_on">HDR is on and working (peak about {0:0} nits).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_bits_limited">The screen can do {1}-bit but only {0}-bit is in use at {2} Hz. The cable or port is limiting it: try a better cable (HDMI 2.1 / DisplayPort 1.4), another port, or a lower refresh rate or resolution.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_bits_hdr8">HDR is on but only {0}-bit color is in use. Gradients may show banding; a better cable or a lower refresh rate usually fixes it.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_bits_ok">{0}-bit color is in use: smooth gradients.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_bits_8">{0}-bit color is in use, which is normal for a standard screen.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_chroma">The picture is sent as {0}. Colors are halved in detail, so small colored text can look blurry. Choose RGB / 4:4:4 in the graphics driver if you can.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Pat_ramp_gray">Gray gradient (banding)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Pat_ramp_dark">Dark gradient (banding)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Pat_ramp_rgb">Red, green, blue gradients</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Pat_black_steps">Black level steps</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Pat_white_steps">White level steps</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Pat_gamut">Color gamut chart</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Pat_white_window">White window</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PatDesc_ramp_gray">A smooth black-to-white ramp. Clear vertical bands mean visible steps; a screen with good processing shows them very faintly.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PatDesc_ramp_dark">Only the darkest 48 levels stretched over the screen, where banding is easiest to see.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PatDesc_ramp_rgb">Red, green and blue ramps, and gray at the bottom. Look for bands or a color tint in the gray ramp.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PatDesc_black_steps">Levels 0 to 15. You should tell every bar from the next one except, on some screens, the first.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PatDesc_white_steps">Levels 240 to 255. If the last bars look like plain white, the screen clips bright detail.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PatDesc_gamut">sRGB, DCI-P3 and Rec. 2020 on the CIE chart, with the triangle Windows reports for this screen drawn dashed.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PatDesc_white_window">A white area on black. Many screens dim it as it grows. Press Up/Down and watch the brightness. It is drawn as ordinary white, so it shows the limiter, not the HDR peak.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Verdict_hdr_on_plain">HDR is on and working.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_PlayHeader">SLIDESHOW</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Slideshow">Slideshow</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_SlideshowTip">Seconds on each picture. Off means you change pictures yourself with the arrow keys.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_ShowHints">Show key hints on screen</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Primary">main screen</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Tips">Turn HDR on or off in Windows first, then press Read again. In the test: arrow keys change the picture, Space runs the slideshow, Up/Down resize the white window, Esc ends.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Group_Gradients">Gradients and banding</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_GroupNote_Gradients">Smooth ramps show visible bands or color tints when a screen or its link can't reproduce them.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Group_Levels">Black and white levels</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_GroupNote_Levels">Near-black and near-white steps show whether dark or bright detail is crushed or clipped.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Group_ColorBrightness">Color range and brightness</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_GroupNote_ColorBrightness">The color triangle Windows reports for this screen, and how brightness holds as a white area grows.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_StartHint">Starts with the first picture, on the screen chosen on the left.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_Start">Start full-screen test</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_NoScreen">No screen to test on. Press Read again.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_MonitorHdr_OpenFailed">Couldn't open the test: {0}</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Intro">Click a picture to open it full screen on the chosen screen. Use the arrow keys to go through them all.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Group_DeadPixels">Dead and stuck pixels</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_GroupNote_DeadPixels">Full-screen colors. A pixel that is wrong shows on at least one of them: a dead pixel stays dark on white, a stuck one stays lit on black.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Group_Backlight">Light bleed and backlight</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_GroupNote_Backlight">Turn the room lights off and look at the edges and corners.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Group_Gradients">Gradients and banding</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_GroupNote_Gradients">Smooth ramps show visible bands or color tints when a display or its settings can't reproduce them.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Group_Calibration">Calibration</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_GroupNote_Calibration">Reference pictures for brightness, contrast, gamma, color and sharpness.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Group_Retention">Image retention and burn-in</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_GroupNote_Retention">Moving the whole picture around helps clear faint after-images.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Group_Motion">Motion</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_GroupNote_Motion">Check blur, trailing and ghosting, and how smooth movement looks.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_black">Black</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_black">Look for bright dots: stuck pixels that stay lit. Best in a dark room.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_white">White</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_white">Look for dark dots (dead pixels) and for yellow, gray or dirty patches.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_red">Red</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_red">Dead or stuck sub-pixels show clearly on pure red.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_green">Green</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_green">Dead or stuck sub-pixels show clearly on pure green.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_blue">Blue</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_blue">Dead or stuck sub-pixels show clearly on pure blue.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_cyan">Cyan</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_cyan">Green and blue together: shows a dead green or blue sub-pixel.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_magenta">Magenta</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_magenta">Red and blue together: shows a dead red or blue sub-pixel.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_yellow">Yellow</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_yellow">Red and green together: shows a dead red or green sub-pixel.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_gray25">Gray 25%</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_gray25">Dark gray: shows uneven brightness, vertical bands and tinted corners.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_gray50">Gray 50%</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_gray50">Mid gray: the best picture for spotting a color tint or uneven panel.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_solid_gray75">Gray 75%</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_solid_gray75">Light gray: shows dirty-screen effect, banding and uneven white.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_bleed_black">Black (light bleed)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_bleed_black">Lights off. Glow along the edges or corners is backlight bleed; soft bright patches are clouding.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_clouding_5">Very dark gray (5%)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_clouding_5">Reveals blotchy, cloudy patches of uneven backlight.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_clouding_10">Dark gray (10%)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_clouding_10">Shows uneven backlight and vignetting a little more gently.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_clouding_20">Dark gray (20%)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_clouding_20">Shows banding and uniformity problems in the darker part of the range.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_ramp_gray">Gray ramp</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_ramp_gray">A smooth gray gradient. You should see no obvious bands and no color tint.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_ramp_red">Red ramp</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_ramp_red">A smooth red gradient. Bands show limited color depth or poor processing.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_ramp_green">Green ramp</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_ramp_green">A smooth green gradient. Look for bands and steps.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_ramp_blue">Blue ramp</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_ramp_blue">A smooth blue gradient. Look for bands and steps.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_steps_gray">Gray steps (16)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_steps_gray">Sixteen gray bars from black to white. Each one should look clearly different from its neighbors.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_black_levels">Black levels</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_black_levels">Sixteen near-black bars. You should be able to tell them from the first one. If the first few look the same, blacks are crushed: raise the brightness a little.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_white_levels">White levels</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_white_levels">Sixteen near-white bars. If the last bars merge into pure white, whites are clipped: lower the contrast.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_gray_steps">Gray steps (11)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_gray_steps">0 to 100% in tens. A good display shows an even progression with no color cast.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_gamma">Gamma</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_gamma">Each box: fine lines (left) beside a solid gray (right). Where the two look the same from a distance, that is your display's gamma. 2.2 is the usual target.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_color_bars">Color bars</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_color_bars">White, yellow, cyan, green, magenta, red, blue and black, with a gray scale below. Colors should be clean and the grays neutral.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_sharpness">Sharpness and text</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_sharpness">Small text and fine lines, black on white and white on black. They should be crisp with no color fringes or blur.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_geometry">Geometry</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_geometry">Grid, circles and corner squares. Circles should be round, grid squares equal, and all four colored corners visible (no overscan).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_checker_1px">1-pixel checkerboard</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_checker_1px">Alternating pixels. It should look like an even gray with no pattern or shimmer. Also shows whether the picture is shown 1:1.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_lines">1-pixel lines</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_lines">Vertical lines (left) and horizontal lines (right). They should be sharp and even.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_cycle_colors">Color cycle</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_cycle_colors">White, black, red, green, blue and gray in turn. Leaving it running for several minutes can clear faint image retention.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_inverse">Inverse bars</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_inverse">Black and white bars that swap every second. Helps even out wear and clear retention (burn-in) on OLED and plasma screens.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_exerciser">Pixel exerciser (flashing)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_exerciser">Fast flashing random colors. It can sometimes free a stuck pixel, but flashing light can trigger seizures in people who are sensitive to it.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_ufo">Motion (UFO)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_ufo">Four boxes at different speeds. Follow them with your eyes: a good, fast display keeps them sharp with little trailing or ghosting.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Pat_scroll_bars">Scrolling bars</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Desc_scroll_bars">Bars moving at 960 pixels per second. Look for blur, trailing or a double image.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_RefreshHeader">Refresh rate</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_RefreshIntro">Measures how often your screen really refreshes and whether frames are being missed. Takes 5 seconds. The figure is for the screen Windows draws the desktop on (normally the main one). With variable refresh (G-Sync, FreeSync) it can read lower while the screen is idle.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Measure">Measure (5 s)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Cancel">Cancel</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Monitor_Reported">Windows says: {0} Hz</system:String>
