    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_GoodToKnow">Good to know</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix1_T">What a Unix timestamp is</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix1_B">The number of seconds since 1 January 1970 at 00:00:00 UTC, called the epoch. It is the same number everywhere on Earth.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix2_T">How it is worked out</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix2_B">Days since 1970-01-01 x 86,400 + seconds into the day. Leap seconds are ignored, so every day is exactly 86,400 seconds.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix3_T">Seconds, milliseconds and more</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix3_B">10 digits are seconds, 13 are milliseconds, 16 microseconds and 19 nanoseconds. The tool picks the unit for you, or you can set it in the sidebar.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix4_T">No time zone inside</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix4_B">A timestamp is one exact moment. The zone only changes how it is shown. A date you type without an offset is read as UTC or local, as you choose.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix5_T">The year 2038 problem</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix5_B">A signed 32-bit counter runs out on 19 Jan 2038 at 03:14:07 UTC (2,147,483,647). 64-bit values last for billions of years.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix6_T">Before 1970 and where it shows up</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Unix6_B">Negative numbers are before the epoch (-86400 is 31 Dec 1969). You will meet timestamps in JWT exp/iat, cron logs, file times and databases.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Zones1_T">Daylight saving changes the offset</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Zones1_B">The same zone can be UTC+1 in winter and UTC+2 in summer. Each card shows the offset that applies on the date you chose.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Zones2_T">Zones have names, not just offsets</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Zones2_B">Ids like Europe/Madrid or America/Mexico_City carry the rules for past and future changes. A bare +02:00 does not.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Zones3_T">Pick a time, not only &quot;now&quot;</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Zones3_B">Type a time and choose its zone to see what it is everywhere else, handy for planning a meeting across countries.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff1_T">Months are not all the same length</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff1_B">The difference is shown as years, months and days, and also as total days, weeks, hours and so on. Use the totals when you need an exact length.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff2_T">Business days</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff2_B">Counts Monday to Friday and, if you keep it ticked, skips the non-working event days from Settings.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff3_T">Adding months clamps to the month end</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff3_B">31 January + 1 month is 28 (or 29) February, because February has no 31st. Years follow the same rule for 29 February.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff4_T">Leap years</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Diff4_B">A year is a leap year when it divides by 4, except centuries, unless the century divides by 400. So 2000 was, 1900 was not.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso1_T">ISO 8601 vs RFC 3339</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso1_B">ISO 8601 is the broad standard. RFC 3339 is a stricter profile for the internet: full date, time with seconds, and an offset or Z.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso2_T">What Z and -00:00 mean</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso2_B">Z is UTC, the same as +00:00. In RFC 3339 -00:00 means the offset is unknown, which is different from UTC.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso3_T">Week and ordinal dates</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso3_B">2024-W10-2 is Tuesday of week 10. 2024-065 is the 65th day of 2024. Both name the same day as a normal date.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso4_T">Durations</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso4_B">P1Y2M3DT4H5M6S is 1 year, 2 months, 3 days, 4 hours, 5 minutes, 6 seconds. M before the T is months, after it is minutes.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso5_T">Basic and extended forms</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso5_B">20240305T143000Z is the compact (basic) form of 2024-03-05T14:30:00Z. ISO accepts both; RFC 3339 only the dashed, colon form.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso6_T">Leap seconds and 24:00</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Iso6_B">A :60 second is allowed only at the end of a UTC day. ISO allows 24:00 as end of day; RFC 3339 hours stop at 23.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal1_T">How ISO weeks work</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal1_B">Weeks start on Monday. Week 1 is the one with the first Thursday of the year (the one holding 4 January). A year has 52 or 53 weeks.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal2_T">The week-year can differ</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal2_B">Days at the start or end of a year can belong to the neighboring ISO year: 29 Dec 2025 is already 2026-W01.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal3_T">US and regional weeks</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal3_B">In the US a week starts on Sunday and week 1 holds 1 January. The &quot;your region&quot; line follows your Windows settings.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal4_T">Day of the year</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal4_B">Counts from 1 on 1 January up to 365, or 366 in a leap year. Used in logs, satellite data and the ordinal form 2024-065.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal5_T">Your event days</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_Fact_Cal5_B">Holidays and special days from Settings appear on their date, and non-working ones are skipped when counting business days.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Settings_NavEventDays">Event days</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Settings_EventDaysIntro">Holidays and days that matter to you. The Date &amp; Time Toolkit names them on their date (Week &amp; day of year), lists the next ones, and skips the non-working ones when it counts or adds business days.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_Settings_NewEventNameHint">Event name, e.g. Company day</system:String>
