    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_ModeIso">ISO 8601 / RFC 3339</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_ModeCalendar">Week &amp; day of year</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_SkipEventDays">Skip my non-working event days</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_SkipEventDaysTip">Business-day counts and &quot;add working days&quot; also skip the non-working days you keep in Settings &gt; Event days.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_EventDaysSkipped">{0} non-working day(s) from Settings are skipped.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_EventDaysInSettings">{0} event day(s) in Settings.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoHeader">ISO 8601 / RFC 3339</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoNote">Reads dates, week dates (2024-W10-2), ordinal dates (2024-065), date-times, durations (P1Y2M3DT4H) and intervals, in basic or extended form.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoNote2">RFC 3339 is the stricter internet profile: full date, a time with seconds, and an offset (Z or +02:00).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_CalOptHeader">Calendar</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_CalOptNote">Shows the ISO week (Monday first, week 1 holds the first Thursday), the day of the year, the quarter, and your event days.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_CalOptSettings">Add or change event days in Settings &gt; Event days.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoHint">A date, time, duration or interval, e.g. 2024-03-05T14:30:00Z</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoNowTooltip">Fill in the current moment as an RFC 3339 UTC timestamp</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoEmptyTitle">Check and convert ISO 8601 / RFC 3339</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoEmptyHint">Paste a timestamp to see whether it is valid ISO 8601 and valid RFC 3339, and to read it in the other forms. Or try one of these:</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_DateTimeTool_IsoValid">Valid ISO 8601</system:String>
