    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B641_T">Base64 is not encryption</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B641_B">It only changes how bytes are written. Anyone can decode it, so never use it to hide a password or a secret.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B642_T">About a third bigger</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B642_B">Every 3 bytes become 4 characters, so the text is about 33% larger than the data (a little more with line breaks).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B643_T">What the = at the end is</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B643_B">It pads the last group to 4 characters. Many URL and token formats leave it off; this tool reads it either way.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B644_T">URL-safe Base64</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B644_B">It swaps + and / for - and _ so the text can sit in a URL or a file name. JWT tokens use it, without padding.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B645_T">The text encoding matters</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B645_B">Text becomes bytes first. The same words give different Base64 in UTF-8 and UTF-16, so both sides have to use the same one.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B646_T">Line breaks</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_B646_B">E-mail (MIME) and PEM files wrap Base64 at 76 or 64 characters a line. Spaces and line breaks are ignored when decoding.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url1_T">Component or full URL</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url1_B">Use Component for one piece, such as a query value. Use Full URL for a whole address: it keeps : / ? # and &amp; so the link still works.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url2_T">Why some characters are escaped</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url2_B">Characters like &amp; = ? # have a meaning in a URL. Inside a value they have to be written as %26 %3D %3F %23.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url3_T">%20 or +</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url3_B">In form data and query strings a space can be a +. Everywhere else it is %20. Turn the option on only for form data.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url4_T">Accents and symbols</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url4_B">Letters like é or € become UTF-8 bytes first, and each byte becomes %XX. So é is %C3%A9.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url5_T">Don't encode twice</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Url5_B">Encoding an encoded text turns % into %25 (%20 becomes %2520). Full URL mode leaves existing %XX alone.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html1_T">Why escape HTML</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html1_B">&amp;lt; &amp;gt; &amp;amp; and quotes have a meaning in HTML. Writing them as entities makes a page show them as text instead of reading them as code.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html2_T">Two ways to write a number</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html2_B">&amp;#233; and &amp;#xE9; both mean é. Writing every non-ASCII character this way keeps the text plain ASCII.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html3_T">Named entities</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html3_B">Decoding knows the named ones too, such as &amp;nbsp; &amp;copy; &amp;eacute; and &amp;euro;.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html4_T">A first line of defense</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_Html4_B">Escaping user text before putting it in a page stops it being read as code. It is not enough inside scripts, styles or unquoted attributes.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File1_T">What a data: address is</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File1_B">It puts a whole file inside text, like data:image/png;base64,... It can go straight into an image tag or a CSS url().</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File2_T">Keep it small</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File2_B">The text is about a third bigger than the file, so it suits icons, small images and fonts. Files over 30 MB are refused here.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File3_T">Back to a file</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File3_B">Switch to Decode, paste Base64 or a data: address, and save it. Known types (PNG, PDF, ZIP ...) are recognized and named for you.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File4_T">Nothing is uploaded</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_EncTool_Fact_File4_B">The file is read and converted on this PC only. Neither the file nor the text is saved by the app.</system:String>

