    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_MsgExplain">{0} means the lighter color is about {1} times as bright as the darker one. Body text needs at least 4.5 : 1; large text and icons at least 3 : 1.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk1Title">What the number is</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk1Body">It compares how bright the two colors are. 1 : 1 means identical, 21 : 1 is black on white. Higher is easier to read.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk2Title">4.5 : 1 - normal text (AA)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk2Body">The minimum for body text, labels and captions. Below it, people with low vision, tired eyes or a dim screen struggle to read.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk3Title">3 : 1 - large text and parts</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk3Body">Enough for headings of about 24 px (19 px if bold), and for icons, borders and input outlines.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk4Title">7 : 1 - the stricter level (AAA)</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk4Body">Aim for it when text is the main content, such as articles, forms and documents.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk5Title">Try it</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Str_ContrastChecker_Walk5Body">Pick a pair or type two colors and watch the marker move on the scale. If it falls short, press Use it to move the text color to the closest one that passes.</system:String>
